{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Liam, the pertinent information is: mental health: coordinated clinical care; safety: individualized response and re-entry plan; medical: medication managed by family/clinicians; and behaviour: risk is assessed through the established plan. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: sleep disruption and fluctuating energy; sustained workload after absences; crowded, unpredictable settings; and stigma or disciplinary responses to illness. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Liam, the most useful EA actions are to follow the school safety plan and escalate to designated staff; maintain calm, respectful boundaries; document observable facts, not interpretations; and support connection and gradual academic re-entry. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-H-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "e